@@s001 foldRight and foldLeft
@@s002 ora:element entering now,dato:accumulator,ok:result
@@s003 <p><code>foldLeft(List(1, 2, 3, 4), 0, _ + _)</code>. The accumulator starts from <code>z = 0</code>.</p>
@@s004 <p>First element: I calculate <b>right away</b> <code>f(acc, 1)</code> = 0 + 1 and pass the result to the next call.</p>
@@s005 <p>Second: 1 + 2. The accumulator already contains the result of everything I have seen.</p>
@@s006 <p>Third: 3 + 3.</p>
@@s007 <p>Fourth: 6 + 4.</p>
@@s008 <p>List finished: I return the accumulator. No operation was left pending: the call is in tail position, constant space. The brackets open <b>on the left</b>.</p>
@@s009 ora:element visited,due:operation pending,ok:operation closed
@@s010 <p><code>foldRight(List(1, 2, 3, 4), 0, _ + _)</code>.</p>
@@s011 <p>First element: <code>f(1, foldRight(rest))</code>. To add 1 I need the result of the rest, which I <b>do not have yet</b>. The operation stays pending.</p>
@@s012 <p>Same thing for 2: another pending operation.</p>
@@s013 <p>And for 3.</p>
@@s014 <p>And for 4. The stack contains 4 open operations.</p>
@@s015 <p>List finished: <code>case Nil => z</code>. Now I have the first real value: 0.</p>
@@s016 <p>The operations are closed <b>from the last</b>: 4 + 0 = 4.</p>
@@s017 <p>3 + 4 = 7.</p>
@@s018 <p>2 + 7 = 9.</p>
@@s019 <p>1 + 9 = <b>10</b>. It is the sequence "10 9 7 4 0" of slide 10. The brackets open <b>on the right</b>, and the stack has reached n operations: it is not in tail position.</p>
@@s020 dato:piece of the Java loop,ora:piece of the fold
@@s021 ora:foldRight (brackets on the right),due:foldLeft (brackets on the left),ok:result
@@s022 ora:what to compare
@@s023 ora:element,dato:accumulator,ok:result
@@s024 ora:foldRight,due:foldLeft
@@s025 no:wrong,ok:correct
@@s026 no:to avoid,ok:preferred
@@s027 foldRight and foldLeft
@@s028 A <b>fold</b> reduces a list to a single value: you start from an initial value <code>z</code> and combine the elements one at a time with a function <code>f</code>. It is the <code>for</code> loop of functional programming. The two folds differ only in the <b>direction</b> in which they put the brackets.
@@s029 The ingredients
@@s030 Think of how you would sum a list in Java: a variable starting from 0, a loop, a <code>+=</code>. A fold has the same pieces, but without a variable.
@@s031 In Java
@@s032 In the fold
@@s033 For the sum
@@s034 the list to go through
@@s035 <code>List(1, 2, 3, 4)</code>
@@s036 <code>int acc = 0;</code>
@@s037 <code>z</code>, the starting value
@@s038 <code>acc = acc + x;</code>
@@s039 <code>f</code>, how to combine
@@s040 the <code>for</code>
@@s041 the fold itself
@@s042 <code>foldLeft</code> or <code>foldRight</code>
@@s043 Z is the result of the empty list
@@s044 Choose <code>z</code> by asking yourself: "what must the fold of an <b>empty</b> list be worth?" Sum → 0. Product → 1. Length → 0. Constructed list → <code>Nil</code>.
@@s045 From the Java loop to the pieces of the fold
@@s046 foldLeft: the accumulator goes forward
@@s047 foldLeft(List(1, 2, 3, 4), 0, _ + _)
@@s048 foldRight: the operations wait
@@s049 foldRight(List(1, 2, 3, 4), 0, _ + _)
@@s050 With subtraction the result changes
@@s051 With <code>+</code> the order of the brackets does not matter. With <code>-</code> it does: that is why the two folds are really different.
@@s052 foldRight(List(1, 2, 3, 4), 0, _ - _)
@@s053 Brackets on the right. One starts from the innermost.
@@s054 Result of foldRight (checked).
@@s055 foldLeft(List(1, 2, 3, 4), 0, _ - _)
@@s056 Brackets on the left. One starts from <code>z</code>.
@@s057 Result of foldLeft (checked). Different from −2.
@@s058 Brackets on the right, brackets on the left
@@s059 The two signatures, compared
@@s060 foldRight and foldLeft of the course
@@s061 Two types: <code>A</code> the elements, <code>B</code> the result. They can be different: from a <code>List[String]</code> you can obtain an <code>Int</code>.
@@s062 In <code>foldRight</code>, <code>f</code> receives <b>first the element</b>, then the result of the rest.
@@s063 In <code>foldLeft</code>, <code>f</code> receives <b>first the accumulator</b>, then the element. The order is reversed: be careful when you write the lambda.
@@s064 In both, on the empty list the result is <code>z</code>.
@@s065 <code>foldRight</code> calls <code>f</code> <b>after</b> having obtained the result of the rest: the recursive call is inside <code>f</code>, not in tail position.
@@s066 <code>foldLeft</code> computes <code>f(z, a)</code> <b>first</b> and passes it as the new <code>z</code>. The call is in tail position: <code>@tailrec</code> confirms it.
@@s067 Order of the arguments of f and position of the recursion
@@s068 What do I put as arguments? Three examples
@@s069 The procedure is always the same: 1) what is worth on the empty list → <code>z</code>; 2) given the accumulator and an element, how do I update → <code>f</code>.
@@s070 length with foldLeft (exercise 8)
@@s071 The length of the empty list is 0: <code>z = 0</code>.
@@s072 <code>f</code> receives the accumulator (how many I have counted) and the element.
@@s073 To count I am not interested in <b>what</b> is in the element: I discard it with <code>_</code> and add 1.
@@s074 Done. And it is in tail position, unlike the recursive <code>length</code>.
@@s075 reverse with foldLeft (exercise 9)
@@s076 The reverse of the empty list is the empty list. <code>f</code> puts the element <b>in front</b> of the accumulator.
@@s077 First element in front of Nil.
@@s078 The second goes <b>in front</b> of the first.
@@s079 The third in front of everyone.
@@s080 The reversal is not a trick: I visit from the left and always add at the head, so the first visited ends up at the bottom.
