@@s099 Style, from week 2
@@s100 The exercise sheet: "from now on explicit recursion is a bad smell". Use a dedicated function (<code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>…) if it exists; a fold only if those are not enough; hand-written recursion only for non-standard iterations.
@@s101 Five questions to choose the fold
@@s102 The course's preferred style (slide 11)
@@s103 For each exercise the course asks for the best style you know how to use. The slide puts in a row, from "condemned" (makes you fail) to "enlightened" (high grade): variables against values, assignments against value bindings, <code>return</code> against the value of the expression, Any/Object against parametric polymorphism, exceptions against Option or Either.
@@s104 For iteration the scale is long: loops, then tail recursion, then simple recursion, then fold, then dedicated higher-order functions composed together (map, filter, flatMap…). To decide: if, then pattern matching, then a dedicated API. Tail recursion and fold remain right when the exercise asks for them explicitly or when memory (the stack) matters. It is the same rule as the exercise sheet: "from now on explicit recursion is a bad smell".
